WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.430
MARK MIRCHANDANI: There are lots
of ways to host an application.

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:04.680
But how do you know which
makes the most sense for you

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:05.550
and your company?

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:06.960
Join us as we talk
with Thunkable

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.543
about how they made it happen
on this episode of "Stack Chat."

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:14.230
Mike, thanks so
much for joining us.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:16.560
Can you tell us a little more
about what Thunkable does?

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:16.950
MIKE GALLAGHER: Sure.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.170
Thunkable is a platform
that allows you to build

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:21.160
mobile apps without code.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:24.950
So if you're a person
that has an idea,

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:27.570
and you're not an engineer,
which not everyone is,

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:30.840
you can snap some blocks
together, drag some components

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.790
onto the screen, and
bring your idea to life.

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:35.850
And we have about
half a million users

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:37.225
and about a million apps built.

00:00:37.225 --> 00:00:38.100
MARK MIRCHANDANI: OK.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.558
Can you tell us a
little bit about,

00:00:39.558 --> 00:00:42.120
from the architecture
side, how Thunkable managed

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.100
the server-side application
that people then

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:45.775
use to build their
own applications?

00:00:45.775 --> 00:00:46.650
MIKE GALLAGHER: Sure.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:50.040
We started off with a
pretty simple architecture.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.597
We had a small user base.

00:00:51.597 --> 00:00:52.930
Just trying to get it out there.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:56.880
So we had a single instance
connected to a cloud Mongo

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.350
and cloud Redis.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.390
And the database is
scaled automatically,

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:03.220
but that single instance
led to some issues.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:04.599
There was a manual deploy.

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:06.750
So whenever we
deployed, it went down.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:11.640
And also, if you had
an unhandled exception,

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.212
it would bring down
the whole server.

00:01:13.212 --> 00:01:15.420
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So I think
this is a common problem

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.003
that a lot of startups
and small teams

00:01:17.003 --> 00:01:19.830
run into as they try to
focus on the application

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.870
and don't, unfortunately,
spend as much time

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:22.955
on the infrastructure.

00:01:22.955 --> 00:01:25.080
How did your team choose
to address these problems?

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:26.610
MIKE GALLAGHER:
We pretty quickly

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.450
realized we needed
multiple instances,

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:30.330
so we spun up a couple more.

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:33.090
But we were still
doing manual deploys.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:35.610
And so now we had
to do three of them.

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.460
And it was great that we
had less downtime-- that's

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:42.120
super important-- and that the
exceptions weren't taking down

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.480
the entire service, but it
was taking too much time.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.910
So we quickly went
to a Docker build,

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:51.670
which allowed us to
automated build that we

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:52.920
can build on our own machines.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.350
We'd push it to Google
Cloud, and then just

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:57.697
deploy it to each of
the instances manually.

00:01:57.697 --> 00:02:00.030
And then shortly after that,
we realized that Kubernetes

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:01.240
could do this for us.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:04.950
So we just allow Kubernetes
to deploy our images to as

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:06.760
many instances as we need.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.310
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So now you're
using managed infrastructure.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:09.852
And you get all the
benefits of that,

00:02:09.852 --> 00:02:12.330
but it sounds like you're
still doing the build manually.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:13.330
MIKE GALLAGHER: Correct.

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:15.330
It took about a half an
on our local machines.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:19.320
So we took a look at a Google
Cloud Build, and we got it up

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:20.070
and running there.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.383
And it wasn't
necessarily faster,

00:02:22.383 --> 00:02:23.925
but we weren't
sitting there watching

00:02:23.925 --> 00:02:26.540
it, which is basically faster.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:29.340
So the other thing was
before, we weren't exactly

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.020
sure what was on production.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.240
It was kind of up
to us to make sure

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.250
that we deployed
the right stuff.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.720
But now we just tag
our master branch,

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.380
and then Google Cloud Build
kicks off and deploys it

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.220
to our staging environment.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:43.635
So it's quite a bit easier.

00:02:43.635 --> 00:02:45.510
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So it
sounds like your team

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:48.430
learned a lot over time and
then implemented those changes.

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:51.600
But if you knew everything you
knew now back then, what would

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:52.683
you have done differently?

00:02:52.683 --> 00:02:55.183
MIKE GALLAGHER: The first thing
I definitely would have done

00:02:55.183 --> 00:02:57.060
was just started with
Docker and Kubernetes.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:00.310
It was nice to live through
the pain points that it solved,

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:02.590
but I could have watched
a three-minute video.

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:05.460
One thing we did do right
was every few weeks,

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:09.900
we'd look at our DevOps process
and see where our pain points

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.520
were and try to address them.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.530
And some of the things
that we've fixed recently

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:17.620
is our Docker build, we've
added Docker layer caching,

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:20.790
so that we can speed up
our builds from, like, 30

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:23.970
minutes to five minutes if
it's just a quick back end fix.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:25.510
So that's been really helpful.

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:28.590
The other thing is making
our Docker image much smaller

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:30.060
has led to faster employments.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.620
And we did that with
multi-stage builds,

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:34.830
which allow the final
product to have just what it

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:36.090
needs to run in production.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:39.840
And having faster deploys
is really important

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.550
when you're constantly
pushing things

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:43.140
and want the ability
to quickly deploy

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:44.730
a hot fix or new features.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:46.980
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Thank you
so much for coming in here

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:50.160
and telling us about how your
team navigated both maintaining

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.170
the application and
improving the infrastructure

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:53.190
at the same time.

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:54.450
MIKE GALLAGHER: Any time.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:56.700
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Want to
try using Google Cloud Build

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.052
and Kubernetes for yourself?

00:03:58.052 --> 00:03:59.760
Check out the tutorial
in the description

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:02.707
to get some hands-on experience.

00:04:02.707 --> 00:04:03.540
Thanks for watching.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:05.790
And make sure to subscribe
for more great Google Cloud

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:06.840
Platform content.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:08.700
We'll see you next
time on "Stack Chat."

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:11.150
